From e9df35fcbc74b428a032a873e7c0d41b274d0808 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:53:45 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] Stereo depth: the lock screen's wallpaper lies at the
 desktop's level

The lock screen's wallpaper is a wallpaper: it sinks to the same level
as the desktop's, while the greeter's clock and password field stay at
the screen. The whole greeter was at the screen's level (Role::Fixed).

The greeter's wallpaper is the surface it places below its own: Qt
Multimedia's stereo layer puts a stereo video wallpaper there, below the
greeter's window (wl_subsurface.place_below), while the interface is the
greeter's own surface and anything Qt puts above it. On a stereo output
with a sunk limit, WorkspaceScene::finalDrawWindow() now draws those
surfaces of a lock screen window at the sunk limit's parallax, cut at the
sides as the desktop is, and the rest of the window at its own level, the
screen's (drawWindowPart() draws a part of a window where it lies, with
the window's paint data). The depth picture of the L + depth outputs puts
them at the same depth. Such a window is drawn per eye, so its opaque
region no longer hides the columns that are cut.

A 2D wallpaper that the greeter draws into its own surface, with the
interface, is not told apart here and stays at the screen's level.

The screen locker's connection can now be made by the tests
(WaylandServer::createScreenLockerConnection() is public), and
Test::setupWaylandConnection() takes the client's end of it.
testStereoDepthWallpaper checks a lock screen with a stereo and with a
flat wallpaper below its interface: the wallpaper's columns moved by each
eye's shift and cut, and the interface at the same place in both eyes.

